WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.460
You've got one presentation, two
audiences, and ninety seconds.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:05.100
Which parts do you keep?

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.200
A buyer needs to understand the problem.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.520
An operator needs the walkthrough.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.300
I change the audience in the Gmail brief,

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.000
keeping the result and disclaimer
required.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.780
HardStop reads the brief and uses the
model to rank what's relevant.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:24.740
The buyer gets the problem.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.620
The operator gets the workflow.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.560
The result keeps its required context.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.320
Here's the buyer's version.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.320
Under ninety seconds, with a deck that
follows the same selection.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.800
For the operator, we get a different cut,
still inside the same limit.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.140
This is the finished video, playing
silently.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.160
The clips live in Dropbox.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.420
HardStop copies the Google Slides deck,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.620
then checks that every recording has the
right slide, in the right order.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.460
It downloads the saved video to check it
again.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.200
Then it prepares this Gmail handoff, ready
for review.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:09.560
Nothing is sent.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.040
But what if someone asks for thirty
seconds?

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.620
The required recordings alone already run
past that.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:21.040
HardStop stops, shows what's blocking the
request,

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.320
and keeps the last working version.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.420
To try a different source, I imported
Harbor:

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.460
six new recordings with their own section
names and dependency.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:34.700
After clarifying the brief,

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.060
the same workflow delivered a cut under
sixty seconds.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:39.900
Another fictional example.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.880
HardStop gives each audience the version
they need,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.920
with the video and slides ready to review
together.
